IMPORT prelude

-- Arbitration (International Investment Disputes) Act 1968 (Singapore) -- registering and enforcing an ICSID award in the General
-- Division of the High Court (ss 2(2), 3, 4, 5), the exclusion of the Arbitration Act 2001 (s 8), the Centre's immunities and tax
-- exemptions given the force of law (s 9 with Convention Articles 20, 21(a), 22 and 24), and, from the Convention set out in the
-- Schedule, who may bring a claim (Articles 25 to 27), the tribunal's nationality rules (Articles 37(2)(b), 38, 39), the post-award
-- deadlines and provisional stays (Articles 49 to 52), finality (Article 53) and conciliation costs (Article 61(1)).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation (version in force from 1 April 2022), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/AIIDA1968.txt. The deposit says the revised edition "incorporates all amendments up to and including
-- 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; the latest amendment annotated in the body is [Act 25 of 2021 wef
-- 01/04/2022] (ss 5 and 6). The meta file records the version as current at 1 October 2026.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0143 (subjects/sg/requirements.jsonl): Tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. It asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper
-- question yet. The person it applies to is an investor (or a State) holding, or resisting, an award of the International Centre
-- for Settlement of Investment Disputes. The Act itself is ten sections; most of the substance is in the Convention in the Schedule.
--
-- THE FORCE-OF-LAW SPLIT. s 9(1) gives "the force of law" only to Articles 18, 19, 20, 21(a) (with Article 22 as it applies to
-- 21(a)), 23(1) and 24. The other Articles are set out in the Schedule (s 2(1) defines "Convention" as the Convention "set out in
-- the Schedule") with no like provision. The rules below drawn from Articles 25 to 61 encode the Convention's text as the Schedule
-- sets it out; that they bind in a Singapore court in their own right is NOT something the Act says (ss 4 to 6 refer to the
-- Convention for the award, its certification and stays).
--
-- Not encoded: s 6 rule-making (the Rules of Court themselves were not retrieved), s 7 (Consolidated Fund), s 10 (amending the
-- Schedule), Articles 1 to 17 (the Centre's organs and panels), conciliation procedure (Articles 28 to 35) beyond the costs rule,
-- the conduct of the arbitration (Articles 40 to 48), the replacement and disqualification of arbitrators
-- (Articles 56 to 58), fees (Articles 59, 60, 61(2) beyond its effect), place of proceedings and the final clauses (Articles 62
-- to 75). Interest on a registered award (s 5(b)) is asserted to run but its rate is not in the Act.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions as shown in the example rows. No pipeline,
-- no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Arbitration (International Investment Disputes) Act 1968 -- awards`

§§ `4 -- registration in the High Court`

-- s 4(1): "Any person seeking recognition or enforcement of an award rendered pursuant to the Convention shall be entitled to have
-- the award registered in the General Division of the High Court subject to proof of any matters that may be prescribed".
-- (2): "In addition to the pecuniary obligations imposed by the award, the award shall be registered for the reasonable costs of and
-- incidental to registration."
-- (3): "If at the date of the application for registration the pecuniary obligations imposed by the award have been partly
-- satisfied, the award shall be registered only in respect of the balance, and accordingly if those obligations have been wholly
-- satisfied, the award shall not be registered."
GIVEN `the sum awarded` IS A NUMBER, `the sum already paid` IS A NUMBER, `prescribed matters proved` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the award may be registered` IF
    `prescribed matters proved` AND `the sum already paid` LESS THAN `the sum awarded`

GIVEN `the sum awarded` IS A NUMBER, `the sum already paid` IS A NUMBER, `the reasonable costs of registration` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the sum for which the award is registered` MEANS
    IF `the sum already paid` AT LEAST `the sum awarded`
    THEN 0
    ELSE `the sum awarded` MINUS `the sum already paid` PLUS `the reasonable costs of registration`

§§ `5 and 3 -- effect of registration, and the Government`

-- s 5: a registered award "shall, as respects the pecuniary obligations which it imposes, be of the same force and effect for the
-- purposes of enforcement as if it had been a judgment of the General Division of the High Court given when the award was rendered
-- pursuant to the Convention and entered on the date of registration", and "(a) proceedings may be taken on the award; (b) the sum
-- for which the award is registered shall carry interest; and (c) the General Division of the High Court shall have the same
-- control over the enforcement of the award".
-- s 3: "Sections 4 and 5 shall bind the Government (but not so as to make an award enforceable against the Government in a manner
-- in which a judgment would not be enforceable against the Government)."
-- Article 55 (Schedule): nothing in Article 54 derogates from the law "relating to immunity of that State or of any foreign State
-- from execution".
DECLARE Obligation IS ONE OF
    `to pay money`
    `to restore property`
    `to do or refrain from some other act`

GIVEN `the award is registered` IS A BOOLEAN, `the obligation` IS AN Obligation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the obligation is enforceable as a High Court judgment` IF
    `the award is registered` AND
        CONSIDER `the obligation`
        WHEN `to pay money` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the award is registered` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the registered sum carries interest` IF `the award is registered`

GIVEN `the debtor is the Government` IS A BOOLEAN, `a judgment would be enforceable against the debtor in that manner` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the registered award is enforceable in that manner` IF
    (NOT `the debtor is the Government`) OR `a judgment would be enforceable against the debtor in that manner`

§§ `2(2) and Articles 49 to 52 -- when the award is rendered, and the deadlines that run from it`

-- s 2(2): "An award shall be deemed to have been rendered pursuant to the Convention on the date on which certified copies of the
-- award were pursuant to the Convention despatched to the parties." Article 49(1) says the same. Days below are counted from that
-- date, not from the hearing, the signing or receipt.
--
-- Article 49(2): on "the request of a party made within 45 days after the date on which the award was rendered" the Tribunal may
-- decide an omitted question and "shall rectify any clerical, arithmetical or similar error". "The periods of time provided for
-- under paragraph (2) of Article 51 and paragraph (2) of Article 52 shall run from the date on which the decision was rendered."
GIVEN `days since the award was rendered` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a request for a supplementary decision or rectification is in time` IF
    `days since the award was rendered` AT MOST 45

-- The clock for Articles 51(2) and 52(2) restarts at a supplementary decision under Article 49(2).
GIVEN `there was a supplementary decision` IS A BOOLEAN, `days since the award` IS A NUMBER, `days since the supplementary decision` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days on the clock` MEANS
    IF `there was a supplementary decision` THEN `days since the supplementary decision` ELSE `days since the award`

-- Article 51(1): revision "on the ground of discovery of some fact of such a nature as decisively to affect the award, provided that
-- when the award was rendered that fact was unknown to the Tribunal and to the applicant and that the applicant's ignorance of that
-- fact was not due to negligence". (2): "within 90 days after the discovery of such fact and in any event within 3 years after the
-- date on which the award was rendered". (Three years is taken as a yes/no fact, not counted in days.)
GIVEN `the fact decisively affects the award` IS A BOOLEAN, `the fact was unknown to the Tribunal and the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the applicant's ignorance was negligent` IS A BOOLEAN, `days since discovery` IS A NUMBER,
      `within 3 years of the award` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a revision application is available` IF
        `the fact decisively affects the award`
    AND `the fact was unknown to the Tribunal and the applicant`
    AND (NOT `the applicant's ignorance was negligent`)
    AND `days since discovery` AT MOST 90
    AND `within 3 years of the award`

-- Article 52(1): annulment "on one or more of the following grounds: (a) that the Tribunal was not properly constituted; (b) that
-- the Tribunal has manifestly exceeded its powers; (c) that there was corruption on the part of a member of the Tribunal; (d) that
-- there has been a serious departure from a fundamental rule of procedure; or (e) that the award has failed to state the reasons on
-- which it is based." Nothing else -- not an error of law or of fact.
-- (2): "within 120 days after the date on which the award was rendered except that when annulment is requested on the ground of
-- corruption such application shall be made within 120 days after discovery of the corruption and in any event within 3 years
-- after the date on which the award was rendered."
DECLARE Complaint IS ONE OF
    `the Tribunal was not properly constituted`
    `the Tribunal manifestly exceeded its powers`
    `a member of the Tribunal was corrupt`
    `a serious departure from a fundamental rule of procedure`
    `the award states no reasons`
    `the Tribunal got the law wrong`
    `the Tribunal got the facts wrong`

GIVEN `the complaint` IS A Complaint
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the complaint is a ground for annulment` IF
    CONSIDER `the complaint`
    WHEN `the Tribunal got the law wrong` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the Tribunal got the facts wrong` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the complaint` IS A Complaint, `days on the clock` IS A NUMBER, `days since discovering it` IS A NUMBER,
      `within 3 years of the award` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the annulment application is in time` IF
    CONSIDER `the complaint`
    WHEN `a member of the Tribunal was corrupt` THEN (`days since discovering it` AT MOST 120) AND `within 3 years of the award`
    OTHERWISE `days on the clock` AT MOST 120

-- Article 50(2) (interpretation): the Tribunal "may, if it considers that the circumstances so require, stay enforcement".
-- Articles 51(4) (revision) and 52(5) (annulment): the same discretion, and "If the applicant requests a stay of enforcement of the
-- award in his application, enforcement shall be stayed provisionally until the Tribunal [Committee] rules on such request."
DECLARE Application IS ONE OF
    `interpretation`
    `revision`
    `annulment`

GIVEN `the application` IS AN Application, `a stay was requested in the application` IS A BOOLEAN, `the request has been ruled on` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `enforcement is stayed provisionally as of right` IF
    CONSIDER `the application`
    WHEN `interpretation` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE `a stay was requested in the application` AND (NOT `the request has been ruled on`)

§§ `8 and Article 53 -- no other remedy`

-- s 8: "The provisions of the Arbitration Act 2001 shall not apply to proceedings pursuant to the Convention."
-- Article 53(1): "The award shall be binding on the parties and shall not be subject to any appeal or to any other remedy except
-- those provided for in this Convention."
DECLARE Challenge IS ONE OF
    `an application to set aside under the Arbitration Act 2001`
    `an appeal to the Court of Appeal on the merits`
    `an application for interpretation under Article 50`
    `an application for revision under Article 51`
    `an application for annulment under Article 52`

GIVEN `the challenge` IS A Challenge
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the challenge is open against the award` IF
    CONSIDER `the challenge`
    WHEN `an application to set aside under the Arbitration Act 2001` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `an appeal to the Court of Appeal on the merits` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `Articles 25 to 27 -- who can bring an investment claim`

-- Article 25(2)(a): a natural person who "had the nationality of a Contracting State other than the State party to the dispute on
-- the date on which the parties consented ... as well as on the date on which the request was registered ... but does not include
-- any person who on either date also had the nationality of the Contracting State party to the dispute".
GIVEN `another Contracting State's national at consent` IS A BOOLEAN, `another Contracting State's national at registration` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `also the host State's national at consent` IS A BOOLEAN, `also the host State's national at registration` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the individual is a national of another Contracting State` IF
        `another Contracting State's national at consent`
    AND `another Contracting State's national at registration`
    AND (NOT `also the host State's national at consent`)
    AND (NOT `also the host State's national at registration`)

-- Article 25(2)(b): a juridical person with "the nationality of a Contracting State other than the State party to the dispute on the
-- date on which the parties consented", and one with the host State's nationality on that date "which, because of foreign control,
-- the parties have agreed should be treated as a national of another Contracting State". Only the consent date counts for companies.
GIVEN `another Contracting State's company at consent` IS A BOOLEAN, `the host State's company at consent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the parties agreed to treat it as foreign because of foreign control` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the company is a national of another Contracting State` IF
        `another Contracting State's company at consent`
    OR  (`the host State's company at consent` AND `the parties agreed to treat it as foreign because of foreign control`)

-- Article 25(1): jurisdiction "shall extend to any legal dispute arising directly out of an investment, between a Contracting State
-- (or any constituent subdivision or agency of a Contracting State designated to the Centre by that State) and a national of another
-- Contracting State, which the parties to the dispute consent in writing to submit to the Centre. When the parties have given their
-- consent, no party may withdraw its consent unilaterally." (3): a subdivision's or agency's consent needs the State's approval
-- "unless that State notifies the Centre that no such approval is required" (not modelled).
GIVEN `a legal dispute arising directly out of an investment` IS A BOOLEAN, `the respondent is a Contracting State or designated agency` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the claimant is a national of another Contracting State` IS A BOOLEAN, `both consented in writing` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the State later withdrew its consent alone` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Centre has jurisdiction` IF
        `a legal dispute arising directly out of an investment`
    AND `the respondent is a Contracting State or designated agency`
    AND `the claimant is a national of another Contracting State`
    AND `both consented in writing`

-- Article 26: consent to arbitration "shall, unless otherwise stated, be deemed consent to such arbitration to the exclusion of any
-- other remedy. A Contracting State may require the exhaustion of local administrative or judicial remedies as a condition of its
-- consent".
GIVEN `the consent preserves other remedies` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the investor may also sue in the host State's courts` IF `the consent preserves other remedies`

-- Article 27(1): no Contracting State "shall give diplomatic protection, or bring an international claim" about a dispute its national
-- has consented to arbitrate "unless such other Contracting State shall have failed to abide by and comply with the award". (2):
-- diplomatic protection does not include "informal diplomatic exchanges for the sole purpose of facilitating a settlement".
GIVEN `the home State's step is only informal exchanges to facilitate settlement` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the host State failed to comply with the award` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the investor's home State may intervene` IF
    `the home State's step is only informal exchanges to facilitate settlement` OR `the host State failed to comply with the award`

§§ `Articles 37 to 39 -- the tribunal`

-- Article 37(2)(b): where the parties do not agree on the number, "the Tribunal shall consist of 3 arbitrators".
-- Article 38: if not constituted "within 90 days after notice of registration of the request has been despatched ... or such other
-- period as the parties may agree, the Chairman shall, at the request of either party ... appoint the arbitrator or arbitrators not
-- yet appointed. Arbitrators appointed by the Chairman ... shall not be nationals of" either State.
GIVEN `the parties agreed a number` IS A BOOLEAN, `the agreed number` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of arbitrators` MEANS
    IF `the parties agreed a number` THEN `the agreed number` ELSE 3

GIVEN `the tribunal is constituted` IS A BOOLEAN, `days since notice of registration` IS A NUMBER, `the period` IS A NUMBER,
      `a party asked the Chairman` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Chairman appoints the missing arbitrators` IF
    (NOT `the tribunal is constituted`) AND `days since notice of registration` GREATER THAN `the period` AND `a party asked the Chairman`

-- Article 39: "The majority of the arbitrators shall be nationals of States other than" the two States, "provided, however, that
-- the foregoing provisions of this Article shall not apply if the sole arbitrator or each individual member of the Tribunal has been
-- appointed by agreement of the parties."
GIVEN `arbitrators` IS A NUMBER, `arbitrators who are nationals of either State` IS A NUMBER, `every member appointed by agreement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the tribunal meets the nationality rule` IF
        `every member appointed by agreement`
    OR  (`arbitrators` MINUS `arbitrators who are nationals of either State`) TIMES 2 GREATER THAN `arbitrators`

§§ `Article 61(1) -- conciliation costs`

-- Article 61(1): "In the case of conciliation proceedings the fees and expenses of members of the Commission as well as the charges
-- for the use of the facilities of the Centre, shall be borne equally by the parties. Each party shall bear any other expenses it
-- incurs". (2): in arbitration the Tribunal decides who pays, "except as the parties otherwise agree", and the decision forms part of
-- the award.
GIVEN `the Commission's fees and the Centre's charges` IS A NUMBER, `the party's own other expenses` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`each party's conciliation bill` MEANS
    `the Commission's fees and the Centre's charges` DIVIDED BY 2 PLUS `the party's own other expenses`

§§ `9 -- immunities and tax exemptions with the force of law`

-- Article 20 (force of law, s 9(1)): "The Centre, its property and assets shall enjoy immunity from all legal process, except when the
-- Centre waives this immunity." s 9(3): a statement of waiver "certified by the Secretary-General of the Centre, or by the person
-- acting as Secretary-General, shall be conclusive evidence of such waiver".
GIVEN `a certified statement of waiver covers the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Centre can be sued` IF `a certified statement of waiver covers the proceedings`

-- Article 21(a): the Chairman, Council members, conciliators, arbitrators, annulment-committee members and Secretariat staff "shall
-- enjoy immunity from legal process with respect to acts performed by them in the exercise of their functions, except when the
-- Centre waives this immunity". Article 22 extends Article 21 to "persons appearing in proceedings under this Convention as parties,
-- agents, counsel, advocates, witnesses or experts". s 9(1) gives force of law to 21(a) and to 22 only "as it applies to Article
-- 21(a)": the 21(b) immigration and national-service privileges are not given force of law.
DECLARE Person IS ONE OF
    `an arbitrator`
    `a conciliator`
    `a member of the Secretariat`
    `counsel for a party`
    `a witness`
    `an expert`
    `a party's director appearing`
    `a journalist reporting the hearing`

GIVEN `the person` IS A Person, `the act was in the exercise of their functions` IS A BOOLEAN, `the Centre waived` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is immune from legal process for the act` IF
    CONSIDER `the person`
    WHEN `a journalist reporting the hearing` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE `the act was in the exercise of their functions` AND (NOT `the Centre waived`)

-- Article 24(2): "Except in the case of local nationals, no tax shall be levied on" salaries and emoluments the Centre pays Secretariat
-- staff. (3): "No tax shall be levied on or in respect of fees or expense allowances received by persons acting as conciliators, or
-- arbitrators, or members of a Committee ... if the sole jurisdictional basis for such tax is the location of the Centre or the
-- place where such proceedings are conducted or the place where such fees or allowances are paid."
GIVEN `the only link to Singapore is where the hearing was held or the fee was paid` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Singapore may tax the arbitrator's fees` IF NOT `the only link to Singapore is where the hearing was held or the fee was paid`

GIVEN `the employee is a local national` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Singapore may tax the Secretariat salary` IF `the employee is a local national`

-- Article 24(1): the Centre is "exempt from all taxation and customs duties". s 9(2): this is not to be construed as "(a) entitling
-- the Centre to import into Singapore goods free of any customs duty without restriction on their subsequent sale therein; (b)
-- conferring ... any exemption from taxes or duties which form part of the price of goods sold; or (c) ... taxes or duties which are
-- in fact no more than charges for services rendered."
DECLARE Levy IS ONE OF
    `income tax on the Centre's income`
    `customs duty on goods imported for its own use`
    `customs duty on imported goods later sold without restriction`
    `tax built into the price of goods it buys`
    `a charge that is really a fee for a service`

GIVEN `the levy` IS A Levy
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Centre is exempt from the levy` IF
    CONSIDER `the levy`
    WHEN `income tax on the Centre's income` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `customs duty on goods imported for its own use` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
